    <div class="ycs-wrap ycs-question-inner">
      <p class="ycs-eyebrow"><span class="ycs-tag">Chapter 5</span> The question</p>
      <h2 class="ycs-h2 ycs-h2-big" id="ycs-ch5">When you can see everything and explain none of it, what does trust look like?</h2>
      <p class="ycs-copy">
        I went into this week thinking that being able to see everything would settle things, and it mostly didn't:
        you can read every line, the build passed, the links work (click around, I'll wait), and I still can't hand you
        the why, because the machine made the decisions on this page and the machine's explanation of them came out of
        the same machine.
      </p>
      <p class="ycs-copy">
        Google has the same thing going on, with more zeros. It said in April that
        <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/cloud-next-2026-sundar-pichai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">75% of its new code is AI-generated</a>
        and approved by engineers. A study last year put 16 experienced developers on code they knew by heart, let
        them use AI on some tasks and not others, and
        <a href="https://www.theregister.com/2025/07/11/ai_code_tools_slow_down/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">measured them 19% slower while they believed they were 20% faster</a>.
        I'd bet your company shipped something this week that passed every review and that nobody in the building could
        walk you through, and that you could see all of it, if you asked.
      </p>
      <p class="ycs-copy">
        So that's the question I'd put to the room, and the one I'd rather you put to me, since asking them is the whole
        job (I host a show about it, which is a strange thing to be qualified for): when the most important systems in
        your life are completely visible and nobody can explain them, what does trust even look like, the track record,
        or the person willing to sign their name to it? I don't have the answer yet, and the longer version of me not
        having it is <a href={POST}>on the blog</a>.
      </p>
